$DIR/main.p:20:7: p1 holds a view of m1 (m1.Ptr("a"), line 16), which may have been moved into takeMap (its parameter takes a pluto/hash.Map[string, int] by value) at line 18, so p1 cannot be used here
    p1 looks at memory that m1 owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    m1.Ptr("a") at line 16. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be
    used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line
    18 m1 was moved into takeMap (its parameter takes a pluto/hash.Map[string,
    int] by value) on some paths to this point, so the view may look at memory
    that was released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p1 before line 18, or move m1 only after the last use of p1
    Or move a copy at line 18, so that m1 keeps its value:
        m1.Clone()
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:27:7: p2 holds a view of m2 (m2.Ptr("b"), line 25), which was dropped by m2.Drop() at line 26, so p2 can no longer be used here
    p2 looks at memory that m2 owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    m2.Ptr("b") at line 25. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be
    used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At line
    26 m2 was dropped by m2.Drop(), so the view may look at memory that was
    released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p2 before line 26, or drop m2 only after the last use of p2
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
